WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.790
These are two of the most confused tools in all of security.

2
00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:07.890
One letter apart, and even experienced analysts mix them up.

3
00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:13.390
But one simple fact separates them, and it has nothing to do with which one is smarter.

4
00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.930
The whole secret is placement.

5
00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:20.590
Not the signatures they know, not how clever the logic is, not the price.

6
00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:25.560
Just one question: where does the box physically sit, relative to the traffic?

7
00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:32.450
Think of protecting a building. You can put a guard in the only doorway, or mount a camera on the wall that watches it.

8
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:35.980
Same event, a completely different power to act.

9
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.190
The inline system is the guard in the doorway.

10
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:44.830
It sits directly in the path, so every packet must pass through it to reach its destination.

11
00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:52.850
So it can inspect each packet in real time and decide on the spot: allow it, drop it, or reset the connection.

12
00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.670
The malicious payload is stopped before it ever reaches its target.

13
00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:02.690
On a modern next-generation firewall, this is usually just a threat-prevention profile, built right in.

14
00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.410
The out-of-band system is the camera on the wall.

15
00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.360
It sits off to the side, not in the path at all.

16
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:17.300
A network tap, or a switch's span port, feeds it a copy, while the original packets keep flowing to their destination.

17
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:23.280
So by the time it matches the copy and raises an alert, the real packet has already been delivered.

18
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.460
It can tell you an attack happened. It can never stop it. It only ever had a copy.

19
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.770
Now, here's the part that surprises people.

20
00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.390
It is not that one of them has a better brain.

21
00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:37.270
Both can run the exact same detection engine.

22
00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:42.720
Suricata produces identical alerts whether it runs inline or out-of-band.

23
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:50.590
The only field that changes is the alert action: allowed when nothing could be blocked, versus blocked or dropped when it runs inline.

24
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:55.340
And because they share the detection method, they share the same weakness.

25
00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:02.040
A signature broad enough to catch a real attack will sometimes match legitimate traffic that just looks similar.

26
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:08.140
A scanner's banner, a load balancer's health check, an old app using a deprecated protocol.

27
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:13.660
That false positive shows up in both of them, because it comes from the detection, not the placement.

28
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:20.210
So, prevent or detect? The answer was never about buying a smarter product.

29
00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.080
It's about where you put it.

30
00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:29.140
Inline, it stops the attack in real time. Out-of-band, it can only watch it go by and tell you afterward.

31
00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:34.180
One guards the door. The other watches it. Same engine, different job.
